Reply with the updated notes, in Hebrew.
- One line per person, formatted as "<name>: <note>", with the name exactly as it appears in what you received.
- Each note is at most about 25 words and describes lasting traits only: how the person writes (sarcastic, humorous, blunt, formal, long or short messages), the positions they keep coming back to, and their usual role in arguments. Do not record one-time events or what was discussed today.
- Be careful with sarcasm: a person who mockingly repeats the other side's slogans does not hold that view. When you are not sure what someone really thinks, describe only their style.
- Keep an existing note unchanged unless the new chat clearly adds to it or corrects it. Keep the notes of people who did not write this time.
- Add a line for a new person only if they wrote enough to show a clear style or position.
- Use Hebrew letters only for the notes. Never write phone numbers and never use the "@" sign.
- Reply with the lines only, without any preamble or closing remarks.
